! store_then_execute.strap -- program store compare with an OBSERVABLE
! difference, which nothing in the suite had before.
!
! store_close_to_fetch.strap covers store-then-LOAD: a store followed
! by a load of the same word must see the new value. This covers
! store-then-EXECUTE: a store into the word holding the NEXT
! INSTRUCTION, which must be re-fetched and executed in its modified
! form. That path was unexercised, and jb_af1c F212A25 walked straight
! into a livelock in it -- prefetch only ever issued at nextFetch and
! moved FORWARD, so a word invalidated by program store compare at or
! ahead of the instruction counter was dropped and never re-read, and
! the core stalled on it forever.
!
! F212A25 alone cannot guard the fix. It patches effective-address
! bits 12-17, which the shift ignores by definition, so the stale and
! the modified instruction are BOTH no-op shifts: that row proves the
! machine does not hang, not that the re-fetch saw the new word.
! This program makes the two outcomes different.
!
! Same shape as F212A25 -- a CM1111 writing ones into the very next
! instruction's address field -- but aimed at a bit that matters. SRC0
! sits at an EVEN word and SRC1 at the one after it, so the two
! addresses differ only in bit 17, the address field's low bit.
! Setting that one bit turns "L(U), SRC0" into "L(U), SRC1":
!   re-fetch sees the NEW word   -> RESULT = SRC1 = frac 2
!   re-fetch sees the STALE word -> RESULT = SRC0 = frac 1, check fails
! (U) throughout so no normalization can alter the value in transit.

        SLC, (8)40.0
START   CM1111(BU,1), TARGET+.17    ! set the low bit of TARGET's address
TARGET  L(U), SRC0                  ! must EXECUTE as "L(U), SRC1"
        ST(U), RESULT
DONE    BEW, $

        SLC, (8)100.0
SRC0    DD(BU,64), (8)0000000000000000000020    ! fraction 1
SRC1    DD(BU,64), (8)0000000000000000000040    ! fraction 2
RESULT  DD(BU,64), 0
        END, START
